WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.980
Film Courage: We will go back to another viewer-submitted
question and this is from Wicked West Films

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:08.730
(nice name by the way) “What advice would
you give to a writer to make their stories

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:11.880
stronger with more depth and meaning?”

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:14.730
We kind of just talked about meaning a little
bit…

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:20.650
Erik Bork, Author/Screenwriter: Well it’s
going to sound kind of facile but it’s kind

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:29.179
of like these seven elements, to kind of like
hit and explore each of those, is what makes

00:00:29.179 --> 00:00:30.240
things powerful.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:35.780
Like when you make sure that what you’re
writing is a punishing problem, it is a universally

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:40.350
relatable situation for your characters, there
is something original, believable, the stakes

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:43.420
are life-altering for the characters.

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:45.469
All of that is what makes something powerful.

00:00:45.469 --> 00:00:51.480
You also want it to be entertaining and it’s
going to mean something to people if you’re

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:57.510
exploring something that is not just your
surface story plot but is something that has

00:00:57.510 --> 00:00:59.350
some depth for the characters.

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:04.050
It’s like if the characters feel something
deeply and are going through something that

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:09.340
the audience connects with emotionally it
feels like they become bonded (like we talked

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:14.020
about) with the character where they feel
like it’s happening to me.

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:19.210
What they are going through I feel like I’m
experiencing it, that’s what really gives

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:21.820
it a powerful impact.

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:26.939
But all these elements of story are all ways
in which you achieve that because the bigger

00:01:26.939 --> 00:01:31.891
the problem is the more important it is to
solve, the more the character is kind of under

00:01:31.891 --> 00:01:36.900
siege trying to solve it, the more you’re
going to have a better chance of the audience

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:37.900
connecting that way.

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:42.620
So much of it has to do with your choice of
idea and kind of the genre that it’s in

00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:47.880
and the basic premise of your story to make
sure that you’re going to have the ability

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:49.590
to have that powerful impact.

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:55.219
Film Courage: So this Save-The-Cat-Moment
or this relatability, does this have to happen

00:01:55.219 --> 00:01:57.180
in the middle, toward the end, in the beginning?

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:00.930
I mean even in SHARP OBJECTS there is just
this small moment where Amy Adam’s character

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:03.700
stops for this gentleman who is about to cross
[in front of her car] and they have this quick

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:08.200
exchange where she gestures to him to go ahead
[and cross in front] and then he gestures

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:12.420
for her to go ahead and then she nods (she’s
not like stubborn about it where she’s like

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:13.420
“Go!

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:14.420
Go!

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:15.420
Go!”).

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:16.420
You see she’s like…okay, I trust her.

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:18.320
I like her in that moment.

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:20.590
But then there are other moments [further
in the show] where you’re like “Hmmmm?

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:23.030
I not quite sure about this person?”

00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:27.940
But that little moment right there (and it
happens fairly soon) makes her relatable.

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:34.560
I think it does help classically in the beginning
if you’re all about making the character

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:38.210
be somebody that the audience can connect
with in the very opening pages.

00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:44.450
So that can have them being a nice person
like that but it can also be with them having

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:48.000
a problems that we can instantly relate to
and feel something about.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:52.550
So even if they’re not that nice…like
in EIGHTH GRADE…she’s not necessarily

00:02:52.550 --> 00:02:58.060
so nice but she’s going through such terrible
problems that most people can relate to that

00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:00.530
you can’t help but start to get emotionally
connected.

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:04.890
So for me it’s a combination of liability
and the problem.

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:09.330
The less likable they are, the more immediate,
obvious and big the problems need to be for

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:13.670
the audience to have something to grasp on
to emotionally.

00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:17.900
When in doubt it’s also good to be likable
but there are certainly some stories that

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:23.520
succeed with characters that aren’t necessarily
classically likable.

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:29.020
But I can say it can be very counterproductive
if your character starts the movie kind of

00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:33.490
a jerk because you’re trying to arc them
into a nicer person at the end.

00:03:33.490 --> 00:03:37.780
If they don’t have really massive problems
they’re facing that make us relate despite

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:43.160
them being a jerk it can be hard for the audience
to want to engage and stay with in my opinion

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:46.270
a character that is really just kind of unlikeable.

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:51.540
It’s rare that that can work, I’m not
saying never but it’s rare.

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:57.041
And I do see a lot of scripts where that’s
the choice the writer made to make the character

00:03:57.041 --> 00:04:00.680
unlikable to begin with because they are so
focused on character arc.

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:04.830
But it can undermine the story if they are
so unlikable that we have no reason as an

00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:07.100
audience to really stay with them.

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:11.410
It’s not as easy to get an audience to want
to join you on that character’s journey

00:04:11.410 --> 00:04:12.410
as you might think.

00:04:12.410 --> 00:04:15.660
You kind of have to make them want to I think.

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:18.630
Film Courage: So establish that fairly early?

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:20.340
Erik: I think.

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:22.050
Film Courage: Interesting.

00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:25.930
So those kind of first few…first impressions
count.

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:26.970
Erik: Yes.

